# Force CRLF for Windows script files. Without this, git on a Unix
# dev box (or any editor that defaults to LF) would silently convert
# CRLF → LF on commit, breaking cmd.exe / bat files at runtime.
#
# Symptom of a broken bat: cmd prints lines like
#   'foo' is not recognized as an internal or external command
# for tokens that are clearly part of `set` assignments in the file.
#
# NOTE: *.bat files are gitignored, so git never touches them. These
# lines are here for completeness, but the CRLF must also be enforced
# at the file level (see the write-bat-as-binary pattern below).
*.bat     text eol=crlf
*.cmd     text eol=crlf
*.ps1     text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts get LF.
*.sh      text eol=lf

# Python: PEP 8 says LF, but we accept either on read.
*.py      text eol=lf

# YAML / TOML / Markdown: LF.
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
